#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# karhunen_loeve_decomposition

#% text
# `karhunen_loeve_decomposition(data)` gives the pair `[transformed, basis]` of the Karhunen-Loeve transform of the numeric matrix `data` whose rows are the variables.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The basis rows are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix of the rows of `data`, largest eigenvalue first.

#% item
# `transformed` is `basis . data`; `transpose(basis) . transformed` gives the data back.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
karhunen_loeve_decomposition([[1., 2., 4.], [2., 3., 10.]])
#% output
#= [[[2.216487549347941, 3.4873265630290113, 10.757247268725504], [0.2952675796384683, 0.9157256372911374, 0.5306893625254716]], [[0.32519047801420087, 0.94564853566687], [0.94564853566687, -0.32519047801420087]]]

#% cell
let r = karhunen_loeve_decomposition([[1., 2., 4.], [2., 3., 10.]]); dot(transpose(r[2]), r[1])
#% output
#= [[0.9999999999999999, 2.0, 4.0], [2.0, 2.9999999999999996, 10.0]]

